WEBVTT

01:36.127 --> 01:39.386
All right. Welcome, everybody. It is 4 o'clock on the

01:39.886 --> 01:43.287
money on November 4th, and we'll welcome you to our

01:43.416 --> 01:47.302
work session here with City Council in Eagle Mountain. get right started

01:47.802 --> 01:50.678
with our first item. It's a discussion on the strategic,

01:51.369 --> 01:54.504
uh, performance incentive programs, and this is Abby Ivory.

02:00.706 --> 02:03.577
While she's setting up, Abby and I had a discussion and she told me she's

02:03.674 --> 02:06.663
so excited for this. Is Rich back?

02:07.353 --> 02:09.538
Oh, and I was— I thank you for reminding me.

02:10.647 --> 02:13.876
Um, we— Rich called in just a little while ago,

02:13.956 --> 02:17.545
uh, Council Member Wood, and he is ill today and not

02:18.045 --> 02:20.355
able to join us, so we'll excuse him. And remind me, I'll do that again

02:20.468 --> 02:20.678
later.

02:29.255 --> 02:32.633
I'm like, I remember I have to drag it or something like that.

02:50.693 --> 02:54.185
Okay, while he's getting that up and going, I'll go ahead and start kind of

02:54.201 --> 02:56.902
talking a little bit about this here. Oh, looks like he might have gotten it.

02:57.922 --> 03:01.134
Thank you so much for letting me come this afternoon.

03:01.492 --> 03:02.906
That's not it. That's not it.

03:06.670 --> 03:10.430
Well, you want the— you want a presentation,

03:10.470 --> 03:13.530
right? Oh, sorry, I'm repeating. Okay, hold on.

03:14.330 --> 03:17.350
Yeah, this, and then present. We're good.

03:17.850 --> 03:21.310
Okay, thank you, thank you, appreciate it. So thank you so much, um,

03:21.810 --> 03:25.370
for letting me come out here. Um, I am proposing— and this is just really

03:25.870 --> 03:29.310
just to talk about this and what are the possibilities— a strategic industry

03:29.810 --> 03:33.230
performance incentive program. Um, the reason why we are doing this

03:33.730 --> 03:36.630
is because we're looking at diversifying our local industries here in Eagle Mountain, and most

03:37.130 --> 03:41.649
of the when we have companies that are looking at us, they're looking for

03:41.809 --> 03:45.326
certain things that we just don't have, such as rail, freeway access,

03:45.486 --> 03:48.810
airport proximity, and then existing buildings as well.

03:49.420 --> 03:51.989
The other thing that they look at is workforce, which we do have. So we're

03:52.489 --> 03:56.244
trying to find ways to really incentivize companies to come here and invest

03:56.744 --> 04:00.644
in Eagle Mountain. We're really looking at doing things like as a targeted

04:01.144 --> 04:04.582
industry, so only certain types of businesses can do this. And that we're

04:04.599 --> 04:07.561
hoping that by creating a preliminary incentive policy,

04:07.999 --> 04:10.832
that it makes it transparent for our city staff,

04:11.220 --> 04:14.411
the companies looking to locate here, city council, and residents on what's

04:14.911 --> 04:15.254
a possibility.

04:20.020 --> 04:24.032
Our goals for this is really to create local jobs,

04:24.452 --> 04:27.995
build a stronger economy resilient to industry layoffs like we've seen

04:28.189 --> 04:31.426
at Tyson, and create public value. These are things that the

04:31.926 --> 04:33.578
public gets out of having these companies come in.

04:34.798 --> 04:38.824
Our targeted industries are advanced manufacturing, energy development,

04:38.985 --> 04:42.350
life sciences, aerospace and defense. And as

04:42.399 --> 04:46.108
a general rule, we would exclude retail and data centers from any kind

04:46.608 --> 04:47.367
of incentives this way.

04:52.540 --> 04:55.703
Some of the— just a synopsis of the report that I

04:55.784 --> 04:59.512
uploaded was that we have at least 100 full-time jobs in Eagle

05:00.012 --> 05:02.832
Mountain. The average wage would be at least 100% of the county average right now.

05:02.914 --> 05:06.988
It's just over $60,000. They would

05:07.004 --> 05:09.468
have at least $50 million in capital expenditure,

05:10.652 --> 05:13.165
and the project must fall into that targeted industry.

05:15.800 --> 05:18.993
And then this just goes over like the points. So what we do is just

05:19.493 --> 05:22.105
preliminary score these as they come in. So if a project came in and talked

05:22.138 --> 05:25.801
about what they would like to do, we would preliminary score them on jobs creation,

05:25.882 --> 05:28.879
wage level, capital investment, the targeted industry fit,

05:29.284 --> 05:31.909
and then they can get some little bonus points, as I like to call them,

05:32.039 --> 05:35.565
for working with a sustainability and community compatibility,

05:36.078 --> 05:39.783
infrastructure and public benefit, and then also for workforce development.

05:40.248 --> 05:43.247
And then at the bottom there, it says the score determines the proposed tier.

05:43.472 --> 05:46.952
It does not guarantee an incentive or the tier maximum. So this

05:47.064 --> 05:50.913
is just a general guideline. Abby, what's the difference in your mind

05:51.413 --> 05:54.890
between workforce development and jobs created? So jobs created is

05:54.986 --> 05:57.568
just like how many jobs they're bringing in. So a lot of times we'll get

05:58.068 --> 06:02.046
something that says, hey, we're gonna We want to do $1.5 billion CapEx with 2,500

06:02.062 --> 06:06.028
jobs in this type of industry. Workforce development is more

06:06.140 --> 06:09.817
working with the local high school, with the colleges to create internships and

06:09.833 --> 06:13.462
some work-trade programs, things like that. Are they creating, going above and beyond what they

06:13.962 --> 06:16.529
normally have to do? Okay. You could come in and just start a business here,

06:16.850 --> 06:19.869
or you could come in and be a part of the community. That's what we're

06:20.369 --> 06:20.816
trying to encourage with this.

06:24.654 --> 06:28.565
And then these are just kind of like basic scoring thresholds with the jobs creation,

06:28.629 --> 06:31.835
the wage level, capital investment, and then the industry

06:32.335 --> 06:34.624
fit. And again, you can get like those little bonus points.

06:36.003 --> 06:38.808
And then just kind of based upon that with the scores that we have,

06:38.968 --> 06:42.575
what is the maximum tax increment and the maximum amount of years that

06:43.075 --> 06:46.470
they would qualify for? And we're kind of basing this off of what's being

06:46.502 --> 06:50.301
done like at the state level. The state offers up to a 30%. Some of

06:50.510 --> 06:53.856
these other state quasi-agencies such

06:53.905 --> 06:57.433
as MIDA or Utah Inland Port also do something like this and

06:57.449 --> 07:00.524
kind of show it out there. It doesn't mean you're guaranteed for that,

07:00.783 --> 07:03.972
it just gives you an idea of theoretically what you could qualify for.

07:07.499 --> 07:10.323
Did you just— sorry, I'm going to let you jump in here, but did you

07:10.339 --> 07:12.745
want us to kind of work through this as you go, or are you going

07:12.777 --> 07:14.778
to go through it all? Whatever you want to do. If you want me to

07:15.278 --> 07:17.933
just present it, and— or do you want to go slide by slide, or even

07:18.601 --> 07:22.335
I mean, I've read it, so. Okay, so I wonder if we can kind of

07:22.607 --> 07:26.413
go slide by slide then and make sure where the questions are. You want me

07:26.913 --> 07:28.966
to go back? Sure thing. Because they've all seen it and read it, so like—

07:29.175 --> 07:32.723
Okay. On that particular one, well,

07:32.739 --> 07:36.529
yeah, let's just start here, but go ahead, Ben. Okay. You just touched on the

07:36.577 --> 07:40.559
word increment, and I just maybe, I know what the council understands what

07:40.655 --> 07:44.139
increment is, and we understand what increment is, But for the sake

07:44.639 --> 07:48.022
of the public and those who may be watching, increment is because

07:48.679 --> 07:52.177
right now the tax might be a very minimal amount on agricultural

07:52.225 --> 07:56.156
ground. And were it not for this business

07:56.656 --> 08:00.263
coming in, this is all we would collect forever. But because they're coming

08:00.391 --> 08:03.552
in and investing a lot of money into our community, they're bringing a

08:04.052 --> 08:06.857
lot of tax base in, and it's giving a portion of that back to them

08:07.050 --> 08:10.320
to help incentivize them to come into this community. We're not coming up with any

08:10.820 --> 08:14.120
money up front in the community. Correct, yeah. We're not taking any of our existing

08:14.620 --> 08:17.600
resources. This is giving some of that back to incentivize them to come in the

08:18.100 --> 08:21.660
first place, maybe as an incubator, the first one in line, or all that type

08:22.160 --> 08:24.600
of thing. But that's what increment is, and a lot of people don't understand what

08:25.100 --> 08:28.180
increment is. So yes, it's— It would come anyway. It's a post— what I'm proposing

08:28.680 --> 08:32.040
is a post-performance reimbursement of their property taxes. Post-performance.

08:32.140 --> 08:35.340
A portion of it, yeah. So they basically have to show that they

08:35.840 --> 08:39.180
did create the jobs at the wages that we've agreed to, and that they've spent

08:39.680 --> 08:43.440
the money they say they've agreed to spend. So we wouldn't reimburse anything until

08:43.537 --> 08:46.872
that is shown. Right, but I think there's a perception, especially in

08:46.904 --> 08:50.431
the community, that we're giving something away and therefore now we have to

08:50.931 --> 08:53.205
raise taxes. This money would have never been here had they not come in in

08:53.382 --> 08:56.605
the first place. Correct. But because we're a new city, we only have, you know,

08:56.685 --> 08:59.635
only 30 years, we have a lot of greenfield. A lot of the companies really

08:59.651 --> 09:02.938
want some existing buildings, so they have to invest in a

09:03.002 --> 09:06.514
building and infrastructure and everything to get that going because we don't have the money

09:06.546 --> 09:09.512
to front it. So if somebody comes in and says, I want to build a

09:10.012 --> 09:14.047
data center or aerospace, something like that, a manufacturing plant, they have

09:14.079 --> 09:17.428
to spend the tens or hundreds of millions of dollars to get

09:17.928 --> 09:21.225
that built and set up. And then if we do something like this, they're more

09:21.725 --> 09:25.327
incentivized to come because they're spending so much money up front. They're willing to come

09:25.439 --> 09:28.724
here. Pretty much like the talent of our local

09:29.224 --> 09:32.650
residents and workforce is like our biggest asset that we have, but it's

09:33.150 --> 09:36.319
not enough to compete nationally for a lot of things. So it's something like this

09:36.431 --> 09:39.910
to to get an industry started. For instance, data centers, we did that

09:39.958 --> 09:43.148
with the first one, Meta. We're not doing that with any more data centers.

09:43.228 --> 09:46.643
We don't need to. We already have them coming without having to incentivize anything.

09:47.172 --> 09:50.346
But the problem is, is like data centers don't bring tons of high-paying jobs.

09:50.779 --> 09:52.991
And we have a lot of people that are working in the tech industry,

09:53.504 --> 09:56.438
which with the layoffs that have been happening lately between Microsoft,

09:56.470 --> 09:59.916
Google, it just seems like maybe that could be something that affects our

10:00.416 --> 10:03.363
residents. And so we're trying to hedge our bets here and saying, Okay, what are

10:03.863 --> 10:07.743
some of these jobs or industries we can bring into Eagle Mountain

10:08.096 --> 10:11.464
that allow people to live and work here so they don't have to travel the

10:11.964 --> 10:15.346
roads every day? And then also we'll give them the same amount of money that

10:15.394 --> 10:18.394
they're making now outside of the city. And that'll bring more,

10:18.747 --> 10:21.923
you know, get people off the roads. That'll help with that. That'll also help us

10:22.276 --> 10:25.468
have more people spending locally as far as like sales tax dollars coming

10:25.500 --> 10:29.334
in. And then resident happiness. They can live and work here. They can get

10:29.834 --> 10:32.359
up in the mountains. Go skiing. go mountain biking earlier in the day. So this

10:32.423 --> 10:36.818
is really trying to recruit all those people. This is just a temporary program

10:37.318 --> 10:40.585
though. Once we establish an industry, just like we did with the data centers,

10:40.940 --> 10:44.964
we can cut this off and stop it. Right, because were it not

10:45.464 --> 10:49.360
for the first investment into Meta, we wouldn't have the trail of data centers

10:49.860 --> 10:54.090
that have come behind that. So it's—

10:54.219 --> 10:58.110
Yeah, they brought in the infrastructure, the 345-kilovolt transmission

10:58.610 --> 11:01.475
lines, which then allowed the other data centers and Tyson plant to come in.

11:01.523 --> 11:05.281
Correct. And now, even though— even if it's not a data center,

11:05.297 --> 11:08.252
they still look at our transmission lines and say, okay, what do you have available?

11:08.332 --> 11:11.737
How do we tie into that? So because of that investment that Meta

11:12.237 --> 11:16.137
made with us and our agreements to reimburse them for some of their property

11:16.202 --> 11:19.879
taxes for a portion of time, it's now being able to bring some other things

11:19.976 --> 11:23.894
in. We now have the infrastructure to expand on our industries,

11:24.007 --> 11:26.723
and so now we're trying to do work with the recruitment of that.

11:32.840 --> 11:36.528
Uh, one, one, uh, comment. Now this is going through

11:37.028 --> 11:40.571
the document, which is not up on the slides, but I

11:41.071 --> 11:43.674
was just thinking about maybe adding semiconductor manufacturing,

11:44.432 --> 11:47.111
that there's a lot of growth in that space. And I don't know that it's

11:47.611 --> 11:51.710
as water-heavy, which is, is a concern for residents, but But

11:51.743 --> 11:55.017
that can bring a lot of good jobs, like Texas Instruments,

11:55.033 --> 11:58.323
Micron. They're coming to Utah, and I feel like

11:58.823 --> 12:02.207
we should be competing for some of those projects as they

12:02.707 --> 12:06.316
come through. Yes, that would be included under advanced manufacturing, and so we're actually trying

12:06.332 --> 12:09.734
to go after that if they show that they're using low water use. I know

12:09.798 --> 12:13.795
that was an interest of yours, is kind of letting y'all know about everything that's

12:14.295 --> 12:16.684
coming in as far as water use and that kind of thing. But it's not

12:16.764 --> 12:19.942
just how much water they'll be consuming, but it's also what they're putting back into

12:19.974 --> 12:23.581
our system. So I work routinely with Brad and saying, okay, this is what

12:23.597 --> 12:27.061
the intake and outtake would be on our sewer system, and will that work with

12:27.561 --> 12:31.056
what we have? So we're always kind of evaluating things, but this would be something

12:31.444 --> 12:34.824
a little bit different from that. But yes, I agree, semiconductor use would be really

12:35.324 --> 12:38.700
great too, and anything that has to do with all of our other industries already

12:38.733 --> 12:44.011
going on here. So one

12:44.044 --> 12:47.588
of the— I've got a few questions on this, but One question is

12:47.668 --> 12:51.374
this: why do we need a framework to begin with? Because it

12:51.874 --> 12:55.689
seems to me when we have a framework, we're basically putting our cards out and

12:56.189 --> 13:00.212
showing all of our cards to potential applicants, whereas if we don't have a framework,

13:00.742 --> 13:04.174
we keep the cards close to our vest, and then when these potential employers

13:04.271 --> 13:07.928
come to us, they don't know what our gives are.

13:09.003 --> 13:12.050
They have to talk to us. It seems like we have a better negotiating hand

13:12.291 --> 13:15.323
if we just keep this close to our vest. Thank you. giving you some

13:15.821 --> 13:19.976
idea that, yeah, depending on these circumstances, you might be able to

13:19.992 --> 13:22.576
let them know, hey, there might be a potential here, I need to talk to

13:23.076 --> 13:26.138
the counsel, without giving anything away. It seems here that we're just putting

13:26.170 --> 13:29.379
all our cards out and then we lose some of the negotiating power. So I

13:29.879 --> 13:32.909
totally understand where you're coming from because I, you know, own a mortgage company.

13:32.973 --> 13:35.235
You come from the real estate world, and so you're trying to get the most

13:35.735 --> 13:38.846
you possibly can for the lowest amount of price. The difference is, is people are

13:39.346 --> 13:42.295
approaching you and saying, I want to buy that house or buy that business,

13:42.972 --> 13:46.622
What price can I get it at? That's one way of looking

13:46.702 --> 13:50.356
at it, but what really actually happens in the site selector world is they

13:50.709 --> 13:54.458
are viewing us way before they even come and call us. So they're

13:54.490 --> 13:58.385
looking at our sound ordinance. That's why we upped our decibels

13:58.497 --> 14:01.429
for industrial areas. They're looking at our zoning.

14:01.782 --> 14:05.355
If it shows an agriculture zone, they don't even bother with

14:05.388 --> 14:09.041
rezoning requests. They just eliminate us. Thank you. So, and then they

14:09.073 --> 14:13.143
look at incentives. So I think the, the really question

14:13.643 --> 14:17.181
that we're trying to decide here as a body is, yeah, as Abby's

14:17.681 --> 14:20.690
done a really good job and there's site selectors out there that are,

14:21.395 --> 14:25.145
that she's dealing with, and we don't— they might work for multiple different companies

14:25.177 --> 14:28.302
and things like that, but if the zone's not already in

14:28.802 --> 14:32.147
place and there's not some kind of framework that she can work within, they're like,

14:32.163 --> 14:35.819
yeah, we're not looking in your area. So it's more of a Not— I don't

14:36.319 --> 14:38.696
think any of us want to show our cards. What we want to do is

14:39.049 --> 14:44.111
empower Abby to say, hey, if they say, is this something

14:44.611 --> 14:47.501
you guys would do, she can say, well, we can always ask. It's never,

14:47.871 --> 14:51.101
yeah, let's do that. It's, yeah, that's within our framework.

14:51.647 --> 14:55.584
But isn't, Mayor, isn't the RTI overlay zone already the mechanism that

14:56.084 --> 14:58.621
we're using to kind of create a runway? I think that's— it is, but it's

14:58.637 --> 15:01.864
not in place on a lot of those areas. So you still have to request

15:02.364 --> 15:05.473
a zone change. When I am going to these conferences, I've been going like once

15:05.973 --> 15:09.418
a month, I'm going to a lot of like private dinners with specific

15:09.467 --> 15:13.476
site selectors, and I'm asking them these questions. And I specifically wondered

15:13.976 --> 15:16.908
about the ag and RTI overlay zone. I said,

15:17.037 --> 15:20.854
if it's already— if it's zoned agriculture, you know that that's just a placeholder,

15:20.934 --> 15:24.157
right? And he said, no, I don't, and I'm not going to ask. I'm just

15:24.657 --> 15:27.413
going to move on to the next place. They're just not even going to do

15:27.429 --> 15:29.290
that. I'm going to just share some thoughts.

15:30.813 --> 15:35.153
I'm going to wrestle with this, Abby, and not

15:35.653 --> 15:39.734
because it doesn't make some sense, but because I tend to agree with what Councilmember

15:40.234 --> 15:44.049
Whiting says. I don't think we should be projecting

15:44.741 --> 15:48.106
our limits of where we're willing to go with some incentives. Frankly, I don't

15:48.606 --> 15:51.136
think we should be giving a lot of incentives to business that's coming in.

15:52.281 --> 15:55.473
For me, they need to convince us that it's a worthwhile endeavor and that they're

15:55.973 --> 15:59.319
going to bring something of value. say, well, if it's 100 employees, and see them

15:59.819 --> 16:02.941
shut down a plant in Delta and bring 50 employees from Delta so they can

16:03.441 --> 16:06.612
meet that 100 threshold and qualify for an incentive.

16:07.092 --> 16:10.362
For me, that just doesn't make a lot of sense. It has to do something

16:10.875 --> 16:14.000
to answer that but-for question that Ben talked about.

16:14.577 --> 16:17.863
Mm-hmm. That if they don't come, then there

16:17.911 --> 16:22.607
isn't going to be any economic activity. But I frankly think that

16:22.655 --> 16:26.390
we are mature enough and growing beyond where we need to beg people to

16:26.442 --> 16:31.034
come and be a part of our community. I think we have demonstrated that

16:31.066 --> 16:34.634
we are a community that can, that can respond to the

16:35.134 --> 16:38.945
needs of big industry, to the residential developers.

16:39.851 --> 16:43.150
Um, and I'm just unwilling to telegraph that on the front end.

16:43.279 --> 16:45.932
So I, I want to be very candid with you about that. I appreciate that.

16:46.672 --> 16:46.952
Um,

16:50.137 --> 16:53.715
and so I'm, I'm listening and I'm thinking, so how do we do that without

16:54.215 --> 16:57.546
telegraphing it? And the RTI Oberlin overlay makes sense

16:58.046 --> 17:00.851
to me, and perhaps that's what we should do, is identify some areas in the

17:01.351 --> 17:04.847
city where we want to encourage that type of development. And maybe

17:05.347 --> 17:09.098
we create an RTI Overlay Zone 2. But I don't want

17:09.598 --> 17:13.270
it to do— I don't want it to address incentives in any way. That just

17:13.495 --> 17:16.832
maybe mimics the requirements of an RTI

17:16.880 --> 17:20.602
Overlay 1, but it limits the types of uses that

17:20.650 --> 17:24.180
can come. That's where my mind would go, and that for me might be

17:24.680 --> 17:28.108
a little more targeted way to say we want

17:28.608 --> 17:31.699
these types of businesses in this area of the city, and we've already done the

17:32.199 --> 17:36.011
work to create a landing spot for you and an administrative

17:36.043 --> 17:40.051
process that has to follow some of the guidelines

17:40.551 --> 17:42.440
that are already in there. I'm just going to leave that out there for some

17:42.940 --> 17:46.367
thoughts. So I'll say that I completely agree with

17:46.867 --> 17:48.756
you, which is why I was trying to kind of steer that conversation in that

17:49.012 --> 17:52.939
direction to say for her to do her job, then can

17:53.439 --> 17:56.625
we put some guardrails so she's not wasting her time with people

17:57.125 --> 18:00.054
that unless it's X, they wouldn't even come? So like, go to the next slide.

18:00.551 --> 18:04.076
For me, one thing that I would probably wrestle with that

18:04.576 --> 18:07.778
you would say is I'm never going 25 years. Anything— I

18:08.278 --> 18:10.903
mean, we already have master development agreements that, that we want to end at 6

18:11.403 --> 18:15.053
years specifically. So is there a reason we'd ever go above 25

18:15.553 --> 18:18.334
years? There was a time we went 40. But I think that for me,

18:18.350 --> 18:21.835
I can tell Abby, no matter the business, 20 years is my appetite.

18:22.077 --> 18:24.417
Now, if somebody says we've got to have 40 years, she can just say,

18:24.482 --> 18:28.324
well, go somewhere else. So I'm trying to say I

18:28.356 --> 18:31.860
agree with you, but give her an idea

18:31.909 --> 18:34.590
of, as she's at these conferences, say that's a heck no.

18:35.741 --> 18:39.233
Anything under that, we can ask. And Mayor, I think there's tremendous value in

18:39.733 --> 18:42.222
what she just stated to us. I mean, she said, look, these are the things

18:42.722 --> 18:45.721
that I'm wrestling with when I'm out there trying to make contact and to put

18:46.221 --> 18:49.593
Eagle Mountain front and center in front of these site selectors, right? Zoning is a

18:50.093 --> 18:53.212
problem. If it's not zoned correctly, they won't even talk to you. I think that's

18:53.244 --> 18:56.894
something we can address. So what other challenges

18:57.394 --> 19:01.340
are you finding? Because what I'm unwilling to do is to say, here's a template

19:01.840 --> 19:05.203
to come and get the maximum incentives from Eagle Mountain's residents.

19:05.284 --> 19:07.744
No, I'm not going to do that. A lot of it is just that.

19:08.026 --> 19:11.454
having access to rail. I did meet with some rail developers about,

19:11.550 --> 19:14.741
you know, would our valley be a good place to have maybe a

19:15.241 --> 19:18.846
transload facility or something like that? And so we really looked at what the

19:19.346 --> 19:22.277
possibilities would be, and it just doesn't work. We're just too close to Tooele and

19:22.358 --> 19:25.613
the Savage rail yard. So we, you know, we don't have the proximity to a

19:26.113 --> 19:28.932
lot of things that they really want. But so it's just a matter of like

19:29.432 --> 19:33.053
trying to entice people to come here. But— So for me, and this is

19:33.553 --> 19:36.821
a perfect segue into something that has just been hanging, and I know that there's

19:37.321 --> 19:40.620
reasons for it, but this is something we should be addressing in the general plan.

19:41.614 --> 19:45.141
Mhm. When you— can you elaborate

19:45.641 --> 19:48.492
a little bit more on what you mean by that and how we'd address it

19:48.992 --> 19:51.522
in the general plan? Because I had something that I jotted down and I want

19:52.022 --> 19:54.327
to see if it's on the same— Look, in the, in the general plan,

19:54.343 --> 19:57.854
you can identify those areas that you're targeting for that, and we can then bring

19:57.950 --> 20:01.617
those areas forward and, and rezone them so that We're saying

20:01.878 --> 20:05.400
this is a runway and a pathway to land this type of development

20:05.466 --> 20:09.148
in these areas. And I think that you have

20:09.180 --> 20:12.603
to do that from an overall concept of

20:12.779 --> 20:15.687
what does the city look like at build out and which areas are we gonna

20:16.187 --> 20:18.664
target first? And I think you have to target the first area you wanna see

20:19.164 --> 20:22.785
that happen. Hmm. And perhaps you identify some other areas that are

20:22.833 --> 20:26.037
future potential RTI 2.

20:26.133 --> 20:29.510
I don't know what you call it. Look, we're spitballing. I'm spitballing.

20:29.623 --> 20:32.256
I love it. No, it's great. That's what the whole purpose of this is,

20:32.369 --> 20:34.938
is just to talk about what the possibilities are and what you want to see

20:35.438 --> 20:38.326
and what you don't want to see. I actually— so while everyone else was kind

20:38.826 --> 20:42.213
of sharing their thoughts, I jotted down future land use designation

20:42.309 --> 20:45.858
question mark, because one of the things we've

20:46.358 --> 20:49.888
been told by different developers, especially when it comes to commercial business park,

20:49.937 --> 20:53.566
those types of things, they'll come in and say 140 acres

20:53.630 --> 20:57.387
is way too much for this particular land use designation.

20:58.061 --> 21:01.272
And it's not that we expect the full 140 acres to go

21:01.772 --> 21:05.782
into that, it's just that's the approximate area in the city that

21:05.847 --> 21:09.266
we think that this makes sense, and that's based off of the feedback we're getting

21:09.298 --> 21:12.589
from residents. So I do think that I

21:13.089 --> 21:16.586
hear what you're saying about RTI and zoning, and maybe

21:17.086 --> 21:21.305
we need to lean a little bit more into either a secondary zone

21:21.739 --> 21:25.474
for RTI or really push and clarify within

21:25.522 --> 21:27.718
our general plan with our future land use designation.

21:28.455 --> 21:32.060
Um, do you have any follow-up to that? Because I've got another— No, I'm just,

21:32.076 --> 21:35.185
I'm thinking about the RTI as it exists currently. I mean, we could

21:35.685 --> 21:39.175
certainly just get into the RTI and modify the, the approved uses,

21:39.512 --> 21:42.973
right? I mean, you could do that as well, as long as you're okay with

21:43.473 --> 21:46.867
the existing zoning having those uses come in in proximity

21:46.900 --> 21:49.753
to what's already going on in those areas. So go ahead.

21:50.571 --> 21:53.602
Um, one of the things that I was thinking about when I first started on

21:53.634 --> 21:56.184
City Council, it was pre-META,

21:57.146 --> 22:00.947
and I was just a teeny tiny little baby City Council member when

22:00.995 --> 22:04.620
that got approved. But the work leading up to META

22:04.844 --> 22:08.308
had been taking place for years before I got onto City

22:08.808 --> 22:12.013
Council. There was extensive research because we really do have

22:12.513 --> 22:15.815
a lot of hurdles as a city

22:15.848 --> 22:19.926
when it comes to attracting business to us. And they looked

22:20.426 --> 22:23.747
at what we could do and they said data centers, because they'll

22:23.796 --> 22:26.605
use some of the water that we have to use up, otherwise it's going to

22:27.105 --> 22:30.588
cost us a fortune. And they're okay with being at the end of the line

22:30.684 --> 22:34.778
where there's not a lot of through traffic. And that

22:35.278 --> 22:38.134
worked out great. The, the city said, we're going to go for this,

22:38.712 --> 22:41.006
very specific. put the incentives there,

22:41.519 --> 22:44.564
and we're starting to reap the benefits from that because we

22:45.064 --> 22:47.594
have data centers coming now without any incentive.

22:48.347 --> 22:52.210
But I— the question that I asked after we got Meta,

22:52.274 --> 22:56.105
and I think after Google was approved, was what

22:56.605 --> 22:59.728
is our next data center? And there's air quotes around that.

23:00.144 --> 23:03.462
So, and I don't think we've ever determined what that is.

23:04.055 --> 23:07.437
And maybe it is advanced air mobility, maybe it is semiconductor

23:09.994 --> 23:14.527
manufacturing. But I think before we do

23:15.027 --> 23:18.482
anything like this or go too crazy, we need to study out what that

23:18.982 --> 23:22.092
next data center type thing is going to be for us in our city.

23:22.768 --> 23:24.380
And I don't think we've figured that out yet.

23:27.250 --> 23:29.666
Who are you having dinner with when you go to these conferences?

23:31.489 --> 23:34.282
When you say industrial, In my head,

23:34.474 --> 23:38.129
I think of driving to the airport through West Valley,

23:38.466 --> 23:41.976
and it's just acres and acres and acres of buildings

23:42.089 --> 23:45.391
and warehouses and things like that. And I'm like, I don't

23:45.407 --> 23:48.725
care about that. Yeah, you don't want that look. I agree with you. You know,

23:48.821 --> 23:52.877
I definitely, when I came on board here, I was told in no uncertain

23:52.909 --> 23:55.971
terms by our lovely recorder, like,

23:56.275 --> 24:00.106
you better keep my lights dim, right? I like my dark skies. And then the

24:00.606 --> 24:03.713
environment was really important, wildlife preservation. And, you know,

24:03.809 --> 24:06.771
being here for a year now, I've really gotten a sense of the community and

24:06.852 --> 24:09.917
how they want things. They want the traffic

24:10.417 --> 24:13.661
off the roads. They want some of these assets like rec buildings and more places

24:13.709 --> 24:15.383
to play and have fun together as a family.

24:16.958 --> 24:21.183
I really think that increasing our jobs

24:21.683 --> 24:24.884
here because of advanced manufacturing and aerospace or something like

24:25.384 --> 24:28.058
that, but make sure you put it out of the way so it doesn't impact

24:28.558 --> 24:32.334
their life. When I do go to these conferences, it's mostly site selectors and they

24:32.834 --> 24:36.891
represent many many different companies, everything from foreign,

24:36.987 --> 24:40.727
domestic investments. Like, I just met with the Korean delegation

24:40.759 --> 24:44.307
last week. And then we have other ones that are doing, like,

24:44.371 --> 24:47.646
food manufacturing. And because it really can impact our sewer, I'm,

24:47.678 --> 24:50.520
like, not too keen on that. We're like, I'm not really into that.

24:51.066 --> 24:54.629
So, but, you know, I really think about what are the wages on

24:54.661 --> 24:58.081
that type of facility. You know, we can't have all high wages.

24:58.434 --> 25:01.018
We kind of need to have the high, the middle, and the lower. That way

25:01.066 --> 25:04.849
we can have a balanced economy here, or you'll deal with a situation that Park

25:05.349 --> 25:07.895
City has where there's so much money that comes in that people can't afford to

25:08.395 --> 25:11.710
live and work there. We don't want that. So we're trying to really get things

25:11.742 --> 25:14.932
diversified. And then looking at the talent of our residents already,

25:14.964 --> 25:18.442
most of them work in tech. How does that translate into other jobs?

25:18.715 --> 25:22.322
Nobody's going to stop working in Lehigh, Silicon Slopes, where they

25:22.822 --> 25:25.896
can make, you know, 6 figures plus a year to come work at, you know,

25:25.912 --> 25:29.141
a meat processing facility here. It's not going to happen. So we need to do

25:29.641 --> 25:32.491
something that will entice them to do better. So a lot of those,

25:33.006 --> 25:36.790
you know, computer tech and computer engineering jobs translate really well over

25:37.290 --> 25:40.703
to aerospace innovation. And so that's what we're looking to do, is trying

25:40.735 --> 25:44.294
to bring that in, because that way people can live, stay here, less people on

25:44.794 --> 25:47.176
our roads. And I'm sure a lot of the residents would appreciate that.

25:48.784 --> 25:52.745
One more thing. Thank you, Mayor, for letting me. I like

25:53.245 --> 25:56.636
that forward

25:56.668 --> 26:00.211
with a conversation about this, because I think we— this is

26:00.711 --> 26:02.969
a nudge for us to have a discussion about what to do with this.

26:03.626 --> 26:06.848
But one thing that I keep going back to, and I had this conversation earlier

26:07.348 --> 26:11.337
today with Councilmember Wright, um, we're putting together

26:11.994 --> 26:15.858
or proposing to put together something like this for the really big fish,

26:16.948 --> 26:20.329
but I see every day the smaller fish that

26:20.829 --> 26:24.372
are trying to make things work, and smaller fish who start in their basements or

26:24.872 --> 26:28.724
their garages and then move to a location here in Eagle Mountain and

26:28.805 --> 26:32.269
then end up having to move to a neighboring city because

26:32.301 --> 26:35.846
there's nothing else for them to move up to when they outgrow their location here

26:36.346 --> 26:39.968
in Eagle Mountain. And I think

26:40.468 --> 26:43.550
we need a mix. I think we need a mix of small and a mix

26:44.050 --> 26:47.651
of big to make everything work. But I also am finding

26:48.151 --> 26:51.711
myself asking, what are we doing for those smaller

26:51.840 --> 26:56.329
businesses, or even the land developers that help facilitate creating

26:56.426 --> 26:58.667
spaces for some of those smaller businesses?

26:59.970 --> 27:03.616
We, um, we talked earlier about incentives and how

27:04.116 --> 27:07.601
they're a reimbursement of an investment in what they're putting into our city.

27:08.275 --> 27:11.846
But I look around at some of our small and mid-sized businesses in our community

27:11.863 --> 27:15.068
and They're the first ones to sign up to sponsor the soccer

27:15.568 --> 27:19.348
jerseys or to sponsor the Turkey Trot or to help with the Wildlife Alliance

27:19.848 --> 27:23.854
or whatever. And, and I think that we would be

27:24.354 --> 27:27.855
doing a disservice to our overall business ecosystem if we didn't

27:27.887 --> 27:31.503
stop and think about what we could be doing as a city to

27:32.003 --> 27:35.503
make it more hospitable for those size businesses too. A lot

27:35.519 --> 27:39.471
of these communities that have a lot of CRAs under the RDAs,

27:39.921 --> 27:43.811
they use the admin fee to create like a revolving loan fund that

27:43.827 --> 27:47.762
can be used to help support smaller businesses. So that is an option to do,

27:47.778 --> 27:51.777
and it's allowed by state statute to do something like that. So, and really a

27:52.277 --> 27:54.588
lot of this is, is, you know, it's, it's hard to kind of create a

27:55.088 --> 27:59.149
company that supports data centers, but could that happen in aerospace or manufacturing

27:59.245 --> 28:02.634
where you have the big ones come in and then all these little things come

28:03.134 --> 28:05.846
in? If you look at what happened, um, 5 years ago, I had to give

28:06.346 --> 28:08.849
a report on like what businesses have come in the last 5 years. So I

28:08.898 --> 28:11.841
just went like Google, You know, satellite back 5 years,

28:12.421 --> 28:16.446
and all of the Macy's Corner didn't even exist. The Maverick,

28:16.526 --> 28:19.682
all those little places and stuff like that. I agree. I used to be a

28:20.182 --> 28:23.247
chamber director as well, so I agree. I'm all for small businesses

28:23.747 --> 28:27.232
and making them thrive. I love to eat at like little mom-and-pop shops, and so

28:27.248 --> 28:30.476
I think that's really important as well. And I'd love to collaborate on some things

28:30.492 --> 28:31.524
to get that going as well.

28:34.350 --> 28:37.844
So, Abby, you're— I mean, your excitement's infectious. I'm grateful that

28:38.344 --> 28:42.309
you're on the team. And I don't want to rein

28:42.341 --> 28:45.618
that in. I know. So I don't want to— I am

28:45.634 --> 28:49.087
not trying to put a cloud on anything, okay? I want

28:49.587 --> 28:53.054
to be clear about that. But what I'm hearing in

28:53.102 --> 28:58.177
this discussion is that there are some holes in our portfolio

28:59.044 --> 29:02.208
that we are unable to put forward to the

29:02.256 --> 29:06.110
business community as whole. And I like what Councilmember

29:06.610 --> 29:10.381
Clark said. You know, maybe we need to take a look at if

29:10.445 --> 29:13.689
we're going to target those types of industries, let's pick one and let's

29:13.769 --> 29:16.868
say let's go find that and let's bring them in. And then we can talk

29:17.368 --> 29:20.866
incentives if we see that that's going to have some value. I also

29:21.366 --> 29:24.318
think zoning is one of the issues I heard you say is a problem and

29:24.415 --> 29:28.076
business space. There's not existing space. Correct. So maybe those are 2 things

29:28.172 --> 29:32.084
that we could start to tackle to create a landing

29:32.132 --> 29:34.439
spot for some of these where you can say, do you know what we just

29:34.939 --> 29:38.557
did? We just rezoned a bunch of property that isn't— that, that with

29:38.589 --> 29:41.953
this type of entitlement, and it allows you this runway to come in and

29:42.453 --> 29:45.607
to start to move your business forward. Or we've been working on

29:45.895 --> 29:48.827
developing some commercial space and we're just looking for the right partner,

29:49.484 --> 29:52.064
and to the right partner we might be able to do X, Y, or Z.

29:52.448 --> 29:55.476
That— I just, I think that would be— It makes sense with what you're saying,

29:55.653 --> 29:59.680
and, and it's one of the things I've been doing, like these and

30:00.307 --> 30:02.701
I talk about the need for flex space,

30:03.296 --> 30:06.734
office space, and then smaller industrial park, and how can

30:07.234 --> 30:10.430
we bring them in. And the problem really lies in they're taking all the

30:10.446 --> 30:14.062
risk with building the building and then not having somebody in

30:14.562 --> 30:17.758
it already. You could have a large warehouse that could

30:18.258 --> 30:20.714
be sitting there for 1 or 2 years, and they're paying interest on that.

30:20.875 --> 30:23.125
And if we're not doing anything to help that along, it's really hurting them.

30:23.173 --> 30:25.730
Thank you. It's just too big of a risk for them. So that's why we

30:26.230 --> 30:29.590
see so many times, and just for residents out there, a lot of times these

30:30.090 --> 30:33.410
little retail squares and stuff like that, they already have businesses

30:33.910 --> 30:36.090
that are going in there and they wouldn't have started them unless they were fully

30:36.590 --> 30:39.990
built out. But if that's a consistent problem, I think that we can look at

30:40.490 --> 30:42.990
that. And that's where you might change my mind about an incentive. If we can

30:43.490 --> 30:45.410
get someone that's willing to come in and take the risk, we can say,

30:45.910 --> 30:48.530
look, we'll take the risk with you for a period of time because the building

30:49.030 --> 30:49.750
is going to be there and that property is going to be there.

30:53.338 --> 30:55.973
And if we need to return a portion of that to take that risk to

30:56.021 --> 30:59.523
start to develop the business community and to provide some of

31:00.023 --> 31:02.640
those things that are missing in our portfolio, I think that's how we could do

31:03.140 --> 31:06.800
it effectively. And I think having— going to these conferences and meeting with site selectors

31:07.300 --> 31:10.576
and talking with them one-on-one and saying, yes, we've done incentives in the past,

31:10.897 --> 31:13.194
we can definitely look at something in the future, is helping.

31:14.270 --> 31:16.921
Some of the times we just get them via email and then have to respond

31:17.421 --> 31:20.646
to them. via email. I have no way to show my nice shiny face here,

31:20.775 --> 31:24.147
you know, happy face, and entice them in that way. So that's the hardest

31:24.647 --> 31:28.048
part, is really getting them in without talking to them. So I might just have

31:28.096 --> 31:31.436
to step up my game when it comes to these conferences and really get

31:31.936 --> 31:34.664
in with them. But just do it, just do a video, just do a little

31:35.164 --> 31:38.662
blurb with you in it. Here's a little invitation from me. Send that out with

31:39.162 --> 31:40.990
the email. They'll be like, who is this woman? I've got to get to know

31:41.490 --> 31:44.618
her in Eagle Mountain City. Yeah, I think for the site selectors it would be

31:45.118 --> 31:48.616
good to know, because they're screening us out, like you said, well before.

31:48.857 --> 31:51.971
They really do. That's what we need to focus on, is what's going to catch

31:52.389 --> 31:55.953
us in their screen where we become a candidate before they ever meet with

31:56.001 --> 31:59.629
us or see you. That if it's seeing an RTI overlay

32:00.129 --> 32:03.402
zone, okay, railroad— I don't really know

32:03.902 --> 32:06.757
that we want railroad, but maybe we do. Don't worry, we're, we're not. But look,

32:06.821 --> 32:09.662
you know, we're not heading down that path. But if there's 3 or 4 things

32:10.162 --> 32:14.201
that would help us screen better, I think we should focus on those areas

32:14.249 --> 32:16.895
so we screen. I think that's what we really need you to bring back,

32:16.943 --> 32:20.455
Abby, or something. Is— so zoning's one. Like, what are the other things

32:20.471 --> 32:22.973
that we're getting, uh, right,

32:23.278 --> 32:27.191
eliminated? I— yeah, some of it was, you know, like overhauling

32:27.207 --> 32:30.655
our website, the economic development, so they can just go on and see

32:31.155 --> 32:33.942
exactly where the sites are. They just— they're like teenagers nowadays. They don't want to

32:34.442 --> 32:36.049
talk to anybody in the real world. They just want to go on and look

32:36.549 --> 32:39.929
at the information. And so we're going to work on that as well. I'll be

32:40.429 --> 32:44.052
in Grand Rapids next week at another site selector conference, and I'll try

32:44.552 --> 32:47.438
and curtail some more of those people and find out, like, how can I improve

32:47.938 --> 32:51.400
it. I've been really grateful because they have done so many evaluations

32:51.900 --> 32:55.143
of what we're offering and how we can improve it to what they are wanting.

32:56.084 --> 32:59.586
So can I— go ahead, sorry. I was

33:00.086 --> 33:03.437
just going to recap. the discussion a little bit. What I, I believe

33:03.485 --> 33:07.001
the direction— what I'm hearing is that the council doesn't want to show your cards.

33:07.434 --> 33:11.304
You want to be more intentional about the direction that you're going, and you

33:11.352 --> 33:14.724
can do that by, uh, going through the general plan and getting

33:14.804 --> 33:18.368
that completed, and also working on zoning that is maybe more targeted

33:18.868 --> 33:22.735
zoning so that you can effectuate the end that you want to try to create

33:22.912 --> 33:26.091
and give Abby the tools that she needs. You want her to keep the excitement

33:26.139 --> 33:29.413
that she has. You don't— you're not trying to stifle that whatsoever. But at the

33:29.913 --> 33:33.055
same time, I'm sure that Abby doesn't want to get ahead of her skis.

33:33.184 --> 33:35.815
I think we've used that term a couple of times. Right. That she wants to

33:36.315 --> 33:39.714
make sure that she's working within the framework that you guys would have. And even

33:40.214 --> 33:43.758
if it may not be something on our website, it's something that you can informally

33:43.854 --> 33:47.047
work with her so that she has good guidance when meeting with others.

33:47.705 --> 33:51.395
And in general, Abby has the

33:51.895 --> 33:55.568
opportunity to reach out to you at any time to get your feedback. and direction

33:56.068 --> 33:59.490
on specific issues, specific cases, and you've always

33:59.522 --> 34:02.624
been very responsive into that. And ultimately,

34:03.845 --> 34:07.364
we want to have a very

34:07.864 --> 34:10.834
strategic, very contemplative, very intentional plan to move forward.

34:11.760 --> 34:15.176
Yes. Can I ask just 2 quick questions about that? Sure.

34:15.257 --> 34:19.029
In terms of checking boxes, can we just not have a— like on our economic

34:19.529 --> 34:22.281
development portion of our website, can't we have something that says, hey,

34:22.853 --> 34:26.627
incentives may be available, Here's sort of the criteria we're looking

34:26.723 --> 34:30.641
at. Contact us for details. Yes, that's what I put out now. Yeah, that is

34:30.738 --> 34:34.415
what I'm doing now. I had hoped that maybe this would be more, you know,

34:34.511 --> 34:37.690
I'm hearing from residents that they want more transparency in what we're doing,

34:37.899 --> 34:40.918
and I thought maybe if we had a framework so they could understand what was

34:40.934 --> 34:44.787
happening, why we're giving it, and what the limits are, that would then help

34:45.287 --> 34:48.641
them understand my job and your job and why we're doing business recruitment and

34:49.141 --> 34:52.093
what kind of things we're doing for it. You know,

34:52.414 --> 34:55.964
ultimately, you see this kind of a framework

34:56.464 --> 35:01.474
in a very mature city that's got a lot of industry and it's grown

35:01.651 --> 35:05.425
and it's— we're kind of that gangly teenager. We're no

35:05.441 --> 35:08.783
longer in the phase of— Speak for yourself. Well,

35:08.799 --> 35:12.172
we're no longer in the phase of saying we want it

35:12.672 --> 35:16.124
all, all the above, we'll take anything. But we're not at that point

35:16.188 --> 35:20.461
where we want to exclude anything. with rigid frameworks

35:20.961 --> 35:24.282
that maybe don't work for us. We want to be intentional and we're still

35:24.314 --> 35:26.739
trying to figure out our arms and our legs and how to move forward.

35:26.867 --> 35:30.785
It's going to take a strong dialogue between the body

35:31.379 --> 35:33.515
and the department. I think it's a perfect game plan.

35:34.350 --> 35:38.107
Thank you. This last question. You say they're

35:38.607 --> 35:41.656
looking at zoning excluding us. Do they not look at the future land use map?

35:42.138 --> 35:46.040
No, not really unless they have a site selected. They're really going— if you

35:46.056 --> 35:49.171
think about it, how many communities across the country They're

35:49.203 --> 35:52.479
looking— they're not just looking in Utah, they're looking all over the

35:52.979 --> 35:56.124
U.S. and what can happen. You know, I've made friendships with the part— with our

35:56.654 --> 36:00.139
partners in the state and other agencies that are

36:00.284 --> 36:03.303
kind of feeding some of these to us. I've noticed they're not coming out in

36:03.335 --> 36:06.595
RFIs. They're actually being, you know, because I'm talking

36:06.643 --> 36:08.763
so much about what I'm doing, I'm putting it out there on social media.

36:08.811 --> 36:11.895
I'm like ad nauseam getting out what we're doing

36:11.943 --> 36:15.669
and how great we are, and that's helping some. But in reality

36:15.950 --> 36:19.073
A lot of it is just coming— some of the information will exclude stuff.

36:19.477 --> 36:22.850
