changes, and these came from Planning Commission, the mayor, or Marcus.

48:33.261 --> 48:36.852
So this section of code right here

48:37.781 --> 48:42.702
is addressing water rights for master development

48:43.202 --> 48:46.651
plans. We wanted to be a little bit more clear, um, with the

48:47.151 --> 48:51.637
language here that— wanted to be clear that res—

48:51.894 --> 48:55.002
that residential water has to— or water for

48:55.502 --> 48:59.640
residential use has to be dedicated to the city at

49:00.140 --> 49:03.517
the time of master development— at the time of the

49:04.017 --> 49:08.184
master development plan and agreement application process. It It essentially said that,

49:08.653 --> 49:12.549
but when we were looking at the language, it was maybe—

49:13.018 --> 49:16.640
could be cleared up a little bit. And our current process is when does

49:17.140 --> 49:20.480
that dedication take place? At C of O?

49:20.544 --> 49:24.546
When does it take place? Yeah, it's currently happening.

49:25.772 --> 49:29.544
There's been uncertainty in that. Sometimes I've seen it happening

49:30.220 --> 49:31.558
at building permit stage.

49:33.761 --> 49:37.109
That's why we're doing this. We're trying to be clear that it's happening

49:38.192 --> 49:41.846
at the master development stage. So we made that change.

49:42.713 --> 49:46.184
We also— actually, one other

49:46.684 --> 49:49.929
thing I didn't mention. I did get feedback

49:50.429 --> 49:53.872
from Brad and John. They also gave me an

49:53.969 --> 49:57.960
additional comment. I tried to

49:58.511 --> 50:01.780
address that. And it was kind of— Brad and

50:02.280 --> 50:06.533
John and the Planning Commission on this section. So the idea— this

50:07.033 --> 50:09.905
used to be called determination of water requirements,

50:10.692 --> 50:14.112
and, and I think it was Brad who's like, hey, we don't want the engineer—

50:14.802 --> 50:18.640
and I completely agree with him— we don't want our city engineer determining the

50:19.140 --> 50:22.815
quantity of water rights or basically preparing the application for the

50:23.315 --> 50:26.571
developer. We want the developer to come in and tell us how much

50:26.683 --> 50:30.033
water they think they're gonna need for the use, and then we'll confirm it.

50:30.514 --> 50:34.073
We'll say, we'll go and do our own calculations and say,

50:34.121 --> 50:37.423
yep, we think that's right, or no, we think you need to

50:37.504 --> 50:41.495
add a little bit more water to your proposal. So this language was

50:41.995 --> 50:45.198
updated to kinda hit home that point, to get rid

50:45.698 --> 50:49.591
of the word identify and use the word confirm, I think is the biggest key

50:50.091 --> 50:53.368
there. Other thing

50:53.465 --> 50:58.005
that we changed, there's just a couple of

50:58.505 --> 51:01.533
things here. Water. Water.

51:01.614 --> 51:05.701
So for non-residential uses like commercial and industrial,

51:08.329 --> 51:11.408
commercial and industrial uses, water comes— would come at

51:11.667 --> 51:15.359
the first permit for like a shell

51:16.751 --> 51:18.419
or just for the primary building.

51:20.857 --> 51:25.192
We want the ability— we're putting in there the

51:25.224 --> 51:29.077
ability to require additional water through the building permit process

51:29.577 --> 51:33.235
if a future tenant comes in and say they went from like— we've got

51:33.735 --> 51:37.088
the game store across the street. If they leave and we have an intensive water

51:37.588 --> 51:40.957
use go in there, we want the ability to say, hey, we need a little

51:41.457 --> 51:45.324
bit more water for this new restaurant coming in or—

51:45.493 --> 51:49.000
Or a salon or whatever. So— Can I jump

51:49.048 --> 51:51.905
in there real quick? Yeah, sure. I think one of the issues that we've run

51:51.953 --> 51:55.371
into quite a bit with— is

51:55.404 --> 51:58.678
that we've historically been requiring this at the tenant improvement level.

51:59.159 --> 52:02.835
Yeah. And instead of at the shell level. And what happens is,

52:02.963 --> 52:06.719
at the shell level, we're having the building permit, we're designing it, maybe designing

52:07.219 --> 52:10.731
for a restaurant. We don't know exactly what type of restaurant or whatever, but we're

52:10.880 --> 52:14.620
we don't— we're not requiring enough water at that shell level. Right.

52:14.720 --> 52:18.160
And then a tenant will come in at the tenant improvement level,

52:18.580 --> 52:22.380
and they're moving forward, and maybe it's

52:22.880 --> 52:26.390
a local restaurant that just is coming in. They'll come through, they have their plan,

52:26.500 --> 52:28.820
they're putting all their improvements in and all this kind of stuff, and all of

52:29.320 --> 52:31.820
a sudden, they get hit with something like, oh my goodness, I need to bring

52:32.320 --> 52:35.320
in 2 acre-feet of water. That's, you know, $50,000, $80,000,

52:35.820 --> 52:39.504
$70,000, whatever. And it totally blows their plan. And then you

52:39.520 --> 52:43.585
see them now opening right-of-way and the stalling out and whatnot, when really

52:44.085 --> 52:47.795
this should have been addressed at the shell level and then making minor tweaks

52:48.295 --> 52:51.201
at the tenant improvement level, not at the shell. You know what I mean?

52:51.314 --> 52:54.415
Yeah. It should be up front. It shouldn't be on that end business,

52:54.753 --> 52:59.171
and suddenly they're carrying a burden and a complete surprise after they've already obtained

52:59.671 --> 53:02.962
the loans, got the franchise agreement, started all the tenant improvements,

53:02.979 --> 53:05.609
and all of a sudden they get hit with something they completely didn't expect.

53:06.234 --> 53:09.906
That— and that's, that's exactly right. And, and part of the reason

53:10.660 --> 53:14.044
that that first section of code that I showed you that we changed, that the

53:14.544 --> 53:18.133
next sentence or 2 that I didn't get into that wasn't changed,

53:18.182 --> 53:22.062
it actually requires— so at an MDP,

53:22.752 --> 53:27.820
an applicant would, through this code, would be required to dedicate

53:28.320 --> 53:32.086
