53:32.230 --> 53:35.892
if they have commercial or industrial or whatever other uses,

53:36.406 --> 53:39.812
they would have to demonstrate to the city through the MDP process

53:40.021 --> 53:43.847
that they have sufficient water so that we don't even get to the point of

53:44.588 --> 53:48.758
building permit and wondering if there is water. We're trying to iron those things

53:48.855 --> 53:52.269
out and require it at the front of the process so we don't end up

53:52.333 --> 53:55.007
with those types of issues. So,

53:57.713 --> 54:01.095
yeah. Can I just jump in a little bit and add some more context?

54:01.600 --> 54:06.136
So with this, I mean, another thing to kind of go back to I think

54:06.636 --> 54:10.166
it was Councilmember Wright that asked the question, but we

54:10.666 --> 54:13.683
need to see these early on in the concept plans and whatnot so that we

54:14.183 --> 54:17.520
can evaluate them against our master plans. 'Cause we need to know what trunk

54:18.020 --> 54:21.037
lines and everything need to go in. And if you have developments coming to come

54:21.133 --> 54:24.537
in, that's what we need to measure it against. And we need to know what

54:25.037 --> 54:29.274
those projects are because if we're gonna go into a master development

54:29.563 --> 54:33.319
plan, With the developer, and they may need to put some

54:33.351 --> 54:36.642
of that infrastructure in to provide it, or they need to

54:37.142 --> 54:40.607
wait till the utilities get to them. Because before,

54:41.153 --> 54:44.669
I think we've ran into scenarios where we've got really far down the road

54:45.169 --> 54:48.746
with projects, they're ready to build or they're building and

54:49.246 --> 54:51.941
we're closing on them, and then we're trying to figure out how to do projects

54:52.118 --> 54:55.055
to facilitate or deliver to them.

54:55.746 --> 55:00.228
So this is really an effort to try to clean up that approach and

55:00.406 --> 55:03.689
just make sure that we're doing our due diligence so that

55:03.738 --> 55:06.762
when somebody comes into the community and they want to land in

55:06.795 --> 55:09.943
the community, they can be successful and we can be successful.

55:12.856 --> 55:16.526
This next change— Yeah, one last thing. Sorry,

55:17.076 --> 55:20.585
I had a request for you to pull that microphone

55:20.634 --> 55:22.605
nice and close because some of the public can't hear you.

55:23.745 --> 55:28.388
Sorry about that. So this

55:28.517 --> 55:32.755
next change here is related

55:32.787 --> 55:36.168
to the priority date for water rights that we'll accept.

55:36.264 --> 55:39.662
It's pretty straightforward. You can see it right there. If it has a

55:40.162 --> 55:43.361
priority date that's prior to 2006,

55:43.377 --> 55:47.185
we'll accept that. If it's not, then we've

55:47.527 --> 55:50.946
seen concerns. Are aware of issues

55:51.043 --> 55:55.060
with the seniority of water rights if they're older

55:55.076 --> 55:58.210
than past that date. We we just want

55:58.710 --> 56:02.372
to be able to put ourselves in position to to receive water rights that

56:03.352 --> 56:07.096
have a better priority date and a better likelihood of not getting

56:07.450 --> 56:10.860
I guess as we've we've kind of talked in staff getting a haircut.

56:10.941 --> 56:14.499
So all this

56:14.547 --> 56:17.766
we're just trying to mature our processes and and put ourselves.

56:17.870 --> 56:20.982
In the best position for sustainable development in

56:21.482 --> 56:24.970
the future. The rest of these, again, this is kind

56:25.470 --> 56:29.595
of a repeat. Some of this language,

56:31.062 --> 56:35.618
I— this is Chapter 1660

56:35.650 --> 56:39.143
related to building codes. And so these sections right here just

56:39.192 --> 56:42.494
kind of get repeated so that we don't miss them when we're reviewing

56:42.593 --> 56:47.180
applications. So that is— those

56:47.357 --> 56:52.027
are the changes. You've had the

56:52.527 --> 56:56.952
code given to you. It is— and

56:57.452 --> 57:00.314
I guess I'll just touch on this for the sake of the audience here.

57:01.009 --> 57:04.860
This application touches

57:05.038 --> 57:08.776
on kind of these main points. One of the

57:09.276 --> 57:13.478
main things in this code Is it? It's saying that any owner

57:13.494 --> 57:17.423
of water and land in an application,

57:17.439 --> 57:20.331
they have to be the applicant. They have to sign the application. We don't want

57:20.347 --> 57:22.773
anybody getting a surprise like, oh my!

57:23.480 --> 57:27.578
I didn't didn't know I was this involved with an application, or that

57:28.078 --> 57:31.934
we just want full investment into into those applications and no surprises

57:32.048 --> 57:35.672
for anybody. Process application or

57:36.172 --> 57:40.104
this proposal. It's going to— it will help

57:40.604 --> 57:44.728
us to go through and help us to

57:45.486 --> 57:49.374
better understand the avail— will help us to understand sooner

57:49.874 --> 57:53.859
in the process the availability of water, how much water is going to be required

57:53.875 --> 57:57.581
for a certain project. Recently, we've been

57:57.695 --> 58:01.671
working with QTS on getting some applications

58:01.703 --> 58:05.219
submitted. And we

58:05.719 --> 58:08.903
ran into an issue of not knowing how much water would be

58:09.403 --> 58:13.489
required for their new buildings.

58:13.586 --> 58:17.257
And so we don't want those

58:17.289 --> 58:21.395
kinds of issues. We're trying to— we want

58:21.895 --> 58:25.343
to be able to catch those issues at the forefront of the application process.

58:26.925 --> 58:30.608
This application This proposal deals with,

58:31.905 --> 58:34.401
sets forth requirements for banking water,

58:36.016 --> 58:38.776
for dedication of water rights to the city.

58:39.454 --> 58:43.053
It sets forth process for maintaining

58:43.166 --> 58:47.079
our records for not only groundwater but CWP water,

58:47.979 --> 58:51.707
and again sets forth when water would be dedicated.

58:51.756 --> 58:55.521
So that's kind of the highlight of what this code,

58:55.795 --> 58:59.179
what's in this code. And I guess I'm here to answer

58:59.679 --> 59:03.080
any questions you might have and turn it over to the developers if they have

59:03.289 --> 59:06.818
additional feedback. So. Brendan, I have

59:06.883 --> 59:10.368
a question. Yeah. I tried

59:10.868 --> 59:14.564
to find the resolution that the council passed restricting the sale of residential water

59:15.064 --> 59:17.068
and I was unable to find it. So somebody's going to have to help me

59:17.568 --> 59:21.028
find that. Yeah. But my recollection is that institutional is not identified

59:21.528 --> 59:24.077
in that. as an approved use. And here,

59:26.078 --> 59:27.672
this seems to contemplate a change.

59:30.006 --> 59:32.418
Right.

59:33.608 --> 59:37.356
Yeah, and that may be— that was, as we discussed it,

59:37.645 --> 59:41.511
we discussed— I was under

59:42.011 --> 59:46.198
no impression that there was any limitation on— as we discussed

59:46.681 --> 59:50.590
it through staff, we didn't feel to limit institutional.

59:50.670 --> 59:53.820
And so I guess you may have pointed out that

59:54.320 --> 59:57.055
we may need to address it, and that's why we're here to get your feedback.

59:57.346 --> 01:00:02.221
Well, and I'm not suggesting that we should or shouldn't, but we haven't.

01:00:02.529 --> 01:00:07.324
And I know that there have been some restrictions placed on at least 2 institutional

01:00:07.824 --> 01:00:10.322
uses in the city. And then the question that I ask, if we're going to

01:00:10.822 --> 01:00:11.051
allow that,

01:00:15.399 --> 01:00:19.142
And that's a, that's a question for the council. But, but my thought process is,

01:00:19.351 --> 01:00:23.147
is if we're going to allow that, then what

01:00:23.758 --> 01:00:27.290
defines it as an institutional use? I, I mean,

01:00:27.793 --> 01:00:31.414
I, I, how do we know that it qualifies in that

01:00:31.479 --> 01:00:35.414
standard? Because I don't— I'm not aware of any language that does that. Maybe it's

01:00:35.462 --> 01:00:38.821
in the code somewhere. We, we don't. I'm not aware of that. I just,

01:00:38.853 --> 01:00:41.580
I want to put that on council's radar. I'm, I'm not I'm not saying it

01:00:42.080 --> 01:00:45.445
should or shouldn't include that, because I have some ambiguous feelings about

01:00:45.945 --> 01:00:49.343
it. Right. But I think if we're going

01:00:49.375 --> 01:00:52.682
to allow it to move forward and to

01:00:52.762 --> 01:00:56.700
be an approved use for the contractual water

01:00:57.200 --> 01:01:00.626
that we have with Central Utah Water Conservancy District, then I think

01:01:00.675 --> 01:01:06.182
that we should at least define what

01:01:06.682 --> 01:01:09.792
qualifies as an institutional use. I think that's a good— thank you.

01:01:10.932 --> 01:01:14.464
My question's pretty easy. On the water analyst, I like that,

01:01:14.769 --> 01:01:18.221
that we're designating somebody for that. Mine was more of

01:01:18.721 --> 01:01:22.524
a budget question. Do you see this being something that will

01:01:22.573 --> 01:01:25.944
increase cost from a staffing budget perspective,

01:01:25.961 --> 01:01:29.814
or will this be added to somebody who's currently already in the budget?

01:01:29.862 --> 01:01:33.122
Does that make sense? We have somebody, John Magnus

01:01:33.234 --> 01:01:37.603
is Okay, I figured. Just wanted for the residents

01:01:37.668 --> 01:01:42.069
to know that. There's no— as far as I know, there's no

01:01:42.085 --> 01:01:45.700
budget change. Thanks, John. I think maybe just

01:01:46.200 --> 01:01:49.892
to add to that though, Councilmember Hewish, is that right now we're taking

01:01:49.957 --> 01:01:52.848
care of it, but as we grow and we mature as a city, as an

01:01:52.912 --> 01:01:57.234
organization, there may be other staffing and other needs that may need to come online.

01:01:57.587 --> 01:02:00.816
But that doesn't mean that it's anything that's going to be proposed right at this

01:02:00.944 --> 01:02:04.124
exact So just want to make that clear.

01:02:04.798 --> 01:02:07.608
I'm going to tell you, I love it. I love the code. I think it's

01:02:08.108 --> 01:02:11.397
really well written. I think it accomplishes the things that I've been concerned about.

01:02:11.622 --> 01:02:13.565
I mean, I've worked with John a little bit on it,

01:02:14.512 --> 01:02:18.591
and I love that we're being proactive and

01:02:18.623 --> 01:02:21.882
that we're planning on the front end to avoid some of those

01:02:21.914 --> 01:02:24.869
hassles or issues that we've been finding on the tail end, right?

01:02:24.981 --> 01:02:29.140
If we can't learn from those things, And that's— I think this represents

01:02:29.205 --> 01:02:33.075
a significant learning curve, and so I'm grateful to staff for working through this.

01:02:34.119 --> 01:02:38.151
I think the only thing I wanted to add to all this is that now

01:02:38.215 --> 01:02:41.732
that we have— or once this gets approved in its final form and

01:02:42.232 --> 01:02:45.073
we have this process in place, I think it gives us a framework as well

01:02:45.362 --> 01:02:48.799
to be able to say, okay, this project hasn't met this threshold

01:02:49.299 --> 01:02:53.120
yet, so it's until they have water or until they're ready to proceed

01:02:53.505 --> 01:02:57.651
we don't have to take our staff time evaluating everything else

01:02:58.151 --> 01:03:01.154
and spending meeting after meeting after meeting going down a path that may never materialize

01:03:01.330 --> 01:03:03.357
if they don't meet this bare minimum requirement.

01:03:06.492 --> 01:03:10.221
Can you go where you first started, that first yellow mark?

01:03:10.269 --> 01:03:15.096
It was number 6. Yeah. On 1640,

01:03:15.225 --> 01:03:18.805
yeah, right there. And just leave that up for a second. I think that's the

01:03:19.305 --> 01:03:22.538
one that that I've heard, there's been some questions, and maybe I

01:03:23.038 --> 01:03:26.770
can do my best to preface staff or council anyway,

01:03:26.850 --> 01:03:30.857
that one of the issues I was brought to my attention

01:03:31.357 --> 01:03:35.185
on this is, say, a large development, and I can pick any large development

01:03:35.685 --> 01:03:39.626
that might have thousands of units. We want that to be master planned,

01:03:40.395 --> 01:03:44.322
and we want for connectivity and trails, and but yet

01:03:44.822 --> 01:03:47.760
if they had to have all the water, we're talking thousands of acre-feet of water

01:03:48.260 --> 01:03:51.314
and millions of dollars at the master plan process,

01:03:51.379 --> 01:03:54.643
yet as it develops out, they might be able to do it on,

01:03:54.756 --> 01:03:58.202
say, a takedown schedule is what we have on some of them. So to require

01:03:58.316 --> 01:04:00.015
that all up front is the question.

01:04:01.802 --> 01:04:05.028
Maybe it needs to be identified, but it can be on more of

01:04:05.528 --> 01:04:08.642
a takedown or a, you know, just that's what

01:04:09.142 --> 01:04:12.306
I heard. And so So I'm

01:04:12.806 --> 01:04:15.325
gonna ask a water rights question that I don't know the answer to.

01:04:16.770 --> 01:04:20.110
What is the process for reserving that? If someone owns those rights, and let's

01:04:20.254 --> 01:04:23.674
say that, I mean, I'm gonna use Firefly as an example. We know

01:04:23.754 --> 01:04:25.890
that they have a lot of units that are approved, they have a lot of

01:04:25.906 --> 01:04:29.037
water. And if they were to bring that in and we're

01:04:29.053 --> 01:04:32.521
like, you have to dedicate 3,000 acre-feet of water to be able to build all

01:04:33.021 --> 01:04:34.320
these units, or 4,000, or whatever it is.

01:04:38.313 --> 01:04:41.255
Because those calculations change, we review those and they change.

01:04:41.320 --> 01:04:43.696
Sometimes they're a little higher, sometimes they're a little lower.

01:04:45.002 --> 01:04:47.734
I, I think that becomes a bit of a question, but I, I think we

01:04:48.234 --> 01:04:52.154
plan for the worst and hope for the best, right? So, so my

01:04:52.654 --> 01:04:57.925
thought process is, how do we— can

01:04:58.038 --> 01:05:01.637
we reserve those rights to be dedicated

01:05:01.669 --> 01:05:04.753
to the city and not require that they're dedicated at, at, at the the

01:05:05.253 --> 01:05:08.165
time that they do it. Maybe it is incremental like the mayor's recommending.

01:05:08.941 --> 01:05:12.288
And does that give us some sort of right to those rights? That's,

01:05:12.353 --> 01:05:16.863
that's my concern. My concern is if the developer continues to control them and

01:05:16.896 --> 01:05:20.261
they haven't dedicated them to the city, are they at risk of going somewhere else?

01:05:21.949 --> 01:05:25.442
Somewhere else within the city, possibly. But with that, I, I want to see

01:05:25.942 --> 01:05:29.387
if, uh, Scott Hazard or, or even Brad, if you guys want to come up,

01:05:29.403 --> 01:05:33.227
maybe help us explain your

01:05:33.308 --> 01:05:35.700
questions on this item. Go ahead.

01:05:37.494 --> 01:05:41.220
Put him on the spot. Yeah. And let us know, you know, help us get

01:05:41.720 --> 01:05:45.268
this right as we're working with developers. Still a discussion.

01:05:45.872 --> 01:05:49.437
You don't get a vote. Neither do I. But we

01:05:49.486 --> 01:05:52.486
want to get it right. I'm well aware, Mayor, that I don't get to vote

01:05:52.986 --> 01:05:57.206
here. So— but I do appreciate you listening. I'm grateful

01:05:57.706 --> 01:06:01.783
to staff. I'm really supportive of bringing structure to this whole water

01:06:02.283 --> 01:06:05.508
world within the city. It's something that's really needed. It has been a little all

01:06:05.573 --> 01:06:09.780
over the map to some extent. And so the overarching

01:06:09.796 --> 01:06:13.586
intent of bringing structure and predictability and trying

01:06:14.086 --> 01:06:16.236
to manage some of these expectations is really well received.

01:06:17.119 --> 01:06:21.632
Mayor, you hit my number one concern, which is what I would classify

01:06:22.132 --> 01:06:24.747
an extraordinary exaction way too early in the process.

01:06:26.948 --> 01:06:29.505
I recognize that water is a scarce resource,

01:06:30.752 --> 01:06:34.377
but you don't require me to bond to prove I have enough money to

01:06:34.409 --> 01:06:38.295
develop the project that you're entitling day one when you approve my master

01:06:38.795 --> 01:06:42.974
plan. That happens at final plat. From a developer

01:06:43.474 --> 01:06:47.109
standpoint, for the private sector, we don't really deploy capital

01:06:47.652 --> 01:06:51.029
to that extent until we know the market's ready to absorb

01:06:51.529 --> 01:06:53.903
it. And we know the market's ready to absorb it when we go to final

01:06:54.403 --> 01:06:57.596
plat. And, you know, take Triumph, for example.

01:06:57.693 --> 01:07:01.257
I'm a small developer. It's 160 acres. It's 234

01:07:01.370 --> 01:07:05.223
units. It's $10 million of water that

01:07:05.723 --> 01:07:08.788
you would say, give that to me day one, and then it takes me 8,

01:07:09.237 --> 01:07:13.011
10 years to absorb that into the market and recover that

01:07:13.511 --> 01:07:17.123
capital expenditure. I'm really supportive of the idea of

01:07:17.623 --> 01:07:21.412
saying, hey, demonstrate that you reasonably have water or

01:07:21.605 --> 01:07:24.271
access to water or a purchase agreement to get water.

01:07:24.978 --> 01:07:28.641
I have several change applications in process that I'm trying to bring water into

01:07:28.689 --> 01:07:32.448
the city to— that's incoming, but it's not ready to dedicate.

01:07:33.508 --> 01:07:38.440
I wouldn't want that timing to hogtie me

01:07:38.633 --> 01:07:41.284
as a developer on when I could develop and what. Thank you.

01:07:42.168 --> 01:07:45.936
And the early dedication, I think, brings into question all sorts of other questions.

01:07:46.032 --> 01:07:49.639
For example, what happens if you change the exaction

01:07:49.671 --> 01:07:52.763
down the road? Do I get a water right credit back for

01:07:53.263 --> 01:07:57.073
what I've already dedicated? So on the residential side,

01:07:57.153 --> 01:08:00.374
I would invite you to reconsider staff's recommendation

01:08:00.874 --> 01:08:04.142
to dedicate water and maybe just demonstrate that we have a reasonable

01:08:04.158 --> 01:08:07.374
path to acquire the water, and then allow us to dedicate it as we go.

01:08:08.617 --> 01:08:12.168
I wouldn't want all of my limited amount of water that I own

01:08:12.424 --> 01:08:16.046
to be pre-dedicated to a single project, and then I

01:08:16.546 --> 01:08:19.668
can't do anything else in the city. Even though I have other residential property,

01:08:19.733 --> 01:08:23.162
I can't develop it because 100% of the water that I had, or most of

01:08:23.662 --> 01:08:27.153
the water I had, went into one project. And then I can't do something

01:08:27.201 --> 01:08:30.199
somewhere else because I can't allocate it where the market is absorbing.

01:08:30.311 --> 01:08:33.773
So those are part of my concerns. So Scott, just to help

01:08:34.273 --> 01:08:37.779
us understand then, when's the right time for that? Is it Demonstrate

01:08:38.279 --> 01:08:41.484
that it's ready at the, at the, uh, master development,

01:08:41.692 --> 01:08:45.221
or demonstrate that it's available, but then dedicate

01:08:45.721 --> 01:08:49.230
it plat by plat as a— Yeah. Final plat.

01:08:49.486 --> 01:08:53.576
So that's still prior to building, prior to building permit, but it's plat

01:08:54.105 --> 01:08:57.425
because, like Triumph as an example, you're gonna have 6 different plats.

01:08:57.874 --> 01:09:01.739
So each plat you dedicate as it goes. Dedicate when I— before I

01:09:01.803 --> 01:09:05.143
record I think that's, that's what we're doing now.

01:09:05.576 --> 01:09:09.157
The water right is dedicated before the recording of that final plat. And one of

01:09:09.657 --> 01:09:11.790
my questions when we get to that is, I know there are concerns at the

01:09:12.290 --> 01:09:16.077
staff level and for council of will that meet the— we have problems

01:09:16.109 --> 01:09:19.433
and issues we're trying to solve. Would that address the issue if we

01:09:19.546 --> 01:09:23.094
did it that way, or do we still create a problem? I mean, part of

01:09:23.594 --> 01:09:26.851
my concern— I love that we're having this conversation, Scott, and thank you for your

01:09:27.351 --> 01:09:31.119
input. It's very helpful. The question that I have is, if you identify

01:09:31.619 --> 01:09:34.567
that I have these water rights and I want to— so I have the ability

01:09:35.067 --> 01:09:38.319
to move forward on this project, and then in the next

01:09:38.819 --> 01:09:41.543
year you bring in another project, you say, I still have these water rights because

01:09:42.043 --> 01:09:45.216
I haven't used them, and this should allow me to be

01:09:45.716 --> 01:09:48.487
able to get this moving forward. I think that's at least one of the holes

01:09:48.583 --> 01:09:51.967
we're going to find on this side of the table. Yeah. And how would

01:09:52.467 --> 01:09:56.300
you address that? What are your thoughts about that? Really simply, Brett, I acknowledge

01:09:56.800 --> 01:10:00.892
the concern. I want that flexibility. How's that for straight talk?

01:10:01.904 --> 01:10:05.903
And I think the private sector should be able to have that flexibility.

01:10:06.079 --> 01:10:08.488
And if I don't have the water, I can't develop it.

01:10:09.371 --> 01:10:13.000
So what did we lose? We lost maybe some time in

01:10:13.500 --> 01:10:16.565
the approval and the consideration process, and maybe I've got to mothball a portion of

01:10:17.065 --> 01:10:20.451
the project of a master plan that I can't deliver until I find the water.

01:10:20.548 --> 01:10:24.056
But I recognize that concern. Thank you. coming in. It's what I do now.

01:10:25.077 --> 01:10:28.477
I allocate water and limited resources, or maybe it's just cash

01:10:28.977 --> 01:10:32.277
and capital or leverage, where I can, where the market

01:10:32.777 --> 01:10:36.298
is absorbing, and where we can recover the investment.

01:10:36.636 --> 01:10:39.855
But to lock it all up into a single investment, I'll never want

01:10:40.355 --> 01:10:43.341
to do a master plan with you. I'll want to break it into small little

01:10:43.841 --> 01:10:46.671
plaques. Section it off and not plan. And just say, here's my little bit that

01:10:47.171 --> 01:10:50.965
I want to do right now. And then that kind of incentivizes this patchwork

01:10:51.465 --> 01:10:54.652
quilt development process instead of a very cohesive,

01:10:54.749 --> 01:10:59.380
well-thought-out master plan. I don't know what the mid

01:10:59.880 --> 01:11:03.220
to long-term outlet is for solving the water issue.

01:11:03.846 --> 01:11:07.120
We're gonna bring in a desalination line from the Pacific Ocean,

01:11:07.412 --> 01:11:10.436
and we're gonna get California to pay for it. That's what we're gonna do.

01:11:11.681 --> 01:11:12.328
You do that.

01:11:15.176 --> 01:11:18.213
I don't want to be in the room when we're raising taxes for that.

01:11:18.950 --> 01:11:21.354
Neither do we.

01:11:21.594 --> 01:11:24.799
That's why California is going to pay for it, because they don't

01:11:25.299 --> 01:11:29.078
seem to mind paying a bunch of taxes. Yeah, I think overall

01:11:29.142 --> 01:11:32.876
staff is— oh, sorry, go ahead. We're having— what

01:11:33.376 --> 01:11:36.338
he's suggesting is what is currently in the code, and the reason that this code

01:11:36.838 --> 01:11:39.750
is coming is because what we— the current process is not working. Well, and that's

01:11:40.250 --> 01:11:43.030
what I'm trying to figure out is like, how do we get it better with

01:11:43.530 --> 01:11:46.750
that? We have about 20,000 vested units, and most of those don't have water that

01:11:47.250 --> 01:11:50.650
can actually support those units. So we have a significant portion

01:11:51.150 --> 01:11:54.770
of our city that is sort of in no man's land when it comes to

01:11:55.270 --> 01:11:58.180
can they develop, can't they develop, is it still vested, is it not vested,

01:11:58.590 --> 01:12:02.630
where you have somebody coming in, getting entitlements,

01:12:03.130 --> 01:12:06.137
selling the property based on the increased value due to getting the entitlements, walking away

01:12:06.637 --> 01:12:10.067
from the project and then with all these promises given to the city and

01:12:10.567 --> 01:12:12.264
then never being able to deliver on them because they don't have the water to

01:12:12.329 --> 01:12:16.483
back it up. And what I'm hearing is we want the flexibility to essentially

01:12:17.061 --> 01:12:20.799
say I'm going to use this water for Project A and B, which is

01:12:20.815 --> 01:12:24.360
not the intent of the ordinance. The intent of the ordinance is to say

01:12:24.745 --> 01:12:27.648
I have the water, here are the water rights. Now I think we can have

01:12:28.148 --> 01:12:31.739
discussions about when's the proper time to dedicate the water, but to say

01:12:32.239 --> 01:12:34.978
I have this water and I have the same water for this Project B as

01:12:35.090 --> 01:12:38.553
well, is not— is completely the opposite

01:12:39.053 --> 01:12:41.406
of what the ordinance is trying to accomplish. It's trying to accomplish saying,

01:12:42.160 --> 01:12:45.847
I am not going to go— you know, I served my mission in the

01:12:46.347 --> 01:12:49.646
former Soviet Union, and when it collapsed, there's a whole bunch of buildings that were

01:12:50.095 --> 01:12:53.462
in partial construction that years later were still in a state

01:12:53.962 --> 01:12:57.084
of partial construction. And it kind of brings you back to the Sermon on the

01:12:57.584 --> 01:13:01.207
Mount, talking about where he saying, who would build not knowing

01:13:01.239 --> 01:13:04.576
whether or not they could complete beforehand,

01:13:04.608 --> 01:13:07.977
unless they say he started to build and could not finish? And that's what we

01:13:08.477 --> 01:13:12.084
end up with, is these entitlements where people start a project and they can't finish.

01:13:13.255 --> 01:13:16.512
That they're, you know, they get the entitlements, they sell it to somebody, and it

01:13:17.012 --> 01:13:19.529
just sits there and sits there and sits there. And I think that's the evil

01:13:20.029 --> 01:13:23.748
that we're trying to address, so to speak, is— and I

01:13:23.764 --> 01:13:27.230
understand the concern is saying, I don't want to dedicate it at preliminary plat or

01:13:27.730 --> 01:13:31.510
at master development agreement stage because, you know,

01:13:31.610 --> 01:13:34.310
that's a lot of money that's tied up before I can ever see a return

01:13:34.810 --> 01:13:38.610
on my investment. I think that's a valid concern. Um, but, um,

01:13:39.110 --> 01:13:43.270
it doesn't look at what the city's concern is, is that we're entitling these projects—

01:13:43.770 --> 01:13:47.030
we're essentially, um, and, and I'm not saying that this is

01:13:47.530 --> 01:13:51.193
Mr. Hazard, but we're essentially incentivizing people to come in to get

01:13:51.209 --> 01:13:54.886
entitlements and then walk away, make a quick buck and walk away. And that is

01:13:54.918 --> 01:13:57.712
something that we don't want to encourage. So my question would be, how do we

01:13:57.728 --> 01:14:01.502
address the idea that if it's a 10-year absorption, and I'm going

01:14:02.002 --> 01:14:04.600
to say it's a 15-year absorption, we have projects that are way longer than that,

01:14:05.259 --> 01:14:08.310
but the master development agreement where we require the water expired

01:14:08.810 --> 01:14:12.243
in 6 years, they only sold 40% of the

01:14:12.743 --> 01:14:14.459
product, but we already have their water.

01:14:17.550 --> 01:14:20.665
No, I understand it's a legitimate concern, but what

01:14:21.165 --> 01:14:24.453
the current— what I hear is a critique of what is being proposed,

01:14:24.890 --> 01:14:27.555
and the solution is to keep the status quo, and we already know the status

01:14:28.055 --> 01:14:32.456
quo doesn't work. Well, the status quo won't work. It's just when can— and

01:14:32.956 --> 01:14:36.741
that's what the discussion is. When is the right time, and how does that—

01:14:38.113 --> 01:14:41.557
So I think that this current proposal

01:14:42.383 --> 01:14:45.495
really helps bring structure to the current way that we're doing it.

01:14:46.217 --> 01:14:49.538
I've had some conversation with staff. I, you know, poor Brandon,

01:14:49.586 --> 01:14:52.490
Brad, I'm like, how are we doing this? Like, we're figuring this out, we're working

01:14:52.990 --> 01:14:55.826
together and just trying to figure out how to, how to prove that we have

01:14:55.859 --> 01:14:59.131
water. Um, you know, this had structures to that to

01:14:59.227 --> 01:15:02.837
demonstrate, you know, how will we demonstrate that we have water, that water

01:15:03.337 --> 01:15:06.495
can be used for development, etc. Um, you know,

01:15:06.511 --> 01:15:09.671
Marcus, I, you know, you're the expert in the room on, you know, what are

01:15:10.171 --> 01:15:13.842
the risks that the city undertakes when you convey vested rights to a developer,

01:15:14.468 --> 01:15:18.432
and how does that relate to whether or not water rights were dedicated?

01:15:18.480 --> 01:15:21.930
I think that's probably the crux of the issue that needs to be considered

01:15:22.430 --> 01:15:25.059
here. But whether I dedicate the water to you,

01:15:25.620 --> 01:15:28.380
or I show you that I have water or access to water,

01:15:29.150 --> 01:15:32.649
you're still encumbering it, and I still have to buy it, or I still have

01:15:32.665 --> 01:15:35.906
to own it. I'm still deploying the capital, because otherwise I

01:15:35.970 --> 01:15:38.698
can't show you that I have the water, unless somebody gave me a 5 or

01:15:39.198 --> 01:15:42.707
10-year takedown period, And any water rights that I know of in this valley that

01:15:43.207 --> 01:15:45.832
are available for sale are available for sale for about 3 minutes.

01:15:46.554 --> 01:15:49.391
And then they're gobbled up, somebody pays cash, and they're gone.

01:15:50.176 --> 01:15:53.847
So, whether I just show you I have water and say it's all for

01:15:54.347 --> 01:15:58.944
this project, but I'm not going to dedicate it now, it's still encumbered

01:15:59.281 --> 01:16:02.727
because I can't allocate it anywhere else. So, is there a better

01:16:03.227 --> 01:16:06.526
way to mitigate the risk without saddling this extraordinary capital

01:16:07.026 --> 01:16:10.606
exaction on the private sector. But that's

01:16:11.106 --> 01:16:13.819
my question. I mean, I don't think we're gonna solve this right now. I think

01:16:14.319 --> 01:16:17.595
we've already spent twice as long as, or 3 times as long as we allocated

01:16:18.095 --> 01:16:21.178
for this in the approximate time. So I mean,

01:16:21.355 --> 01:16:24.826
I do think that it makes sense for us to try

01:16:25.326 --> 01:16:28.602
to work through this outside of a City Council meeting. Thank you.

